@@ -0,0 +1,124 @@
+# Push notification setup (Firebase Cloud Messaging)
+
+Real, OS-level push notifications — the kind that show up in the phone's
+notification tray while the app is closed — are fully wired up in code on
+both ends:
+
+- **Client:** `src/utils/usePushNotifications.js` registers this device
+  the moment someone signs in, using `@capacitor/push-notifications`.
+- **Backend:** `backend/src/lib/push.js` sends the actual push via
+  Firebase Cloud Messaging's HTTP v1 API whenever `notify()` fires (likes,
+  comments, follows, mentions — `backend/src/lib/notify.js`) or a DM
+  arrives (`backend/src/routes/messages.js`).
+
+What's *not* done, and can't be done from this repo alone: the Firebase
+project itself. That's a Google account you'd own, not something that can
+be created or faked on your behalf. Until you do the steps below, every
+push send is a silent no-op — nothing breaks, people just don't get a
+system-tray notification yet (the in-app Activity tab still works
+exactly as it always did).
+
+---
+
+## 1. Create a Firebase project and add the Android app
+
+1. Go to the [Firebase console](https://console.firebase.google.com/) →
+   **Add project** (an existing Google Cloud project works too — Firebase
+   will offer to link it).
+2. Once created, click **Add app → Android**.
+3. **Android package name:** `com.bharatspace.app` (must match exactly —
+   this is `applicationId` in `android/android/app/build.gradle`).
+4. App nickname/SHA-1 are optional for push notifications specifically
+   (SHA-1 only matters if you later add Firebase Auth or Dynamic Links) —
+   skip them.
+5. Download the **`google-services.json`** file it offers you.
+
+## 2. Place the config file
+
+Copy the downloaded file to:
+
+```
+android/android/app/google-services.json
+```
+
+This exact path matters — `android/android/app/build.gradle` already has
+a conditional block that only applies the `google-services` Gradle plugin
+when this file exists:
+
+```groovy
+try {
+    def servicesJSON = file('google-services.json')
+    if (servicesJSON.text) {
+        apply plugin: 'com.google.gms.google-services'
+    }
+} catch(Exception e) {
+    logger.info("google-services.json not found, google-services plugin not applied. Push Notifications won't work")
+}
+```
+
+That's why the Android build has always succeeded even without this file
+— it's designed to degrade gracefully, not to require Firebase setup
+just to compile. `google-services.json` is not committed (it's excluded
+in `.gitignore` alongside `keystore.properties`, for the same reason:
+it's specific to your own Firebase project) — you'll place it locally and
+on whichever machine/CI runner builds your release APK.
+
+## 3. Generate a service-account key for the backend
+
+The backend sends pushes via FCM's HTTP v1 API, which authenticates with
+a service account rather than a legacy server key:
+
+1. Firebase console → ⚙️ **Project settings** → **Service accounts** tab.
+2. Click **Generate new private key** → confirm. This downloads a JSON
+   file (e.g. `bharatspace-firebase-adminsdk-xxxxx.json`).
+3. Set it as a Worker secret — **the entire file contents, as one line**:
+
+   ```bash
+   cd backend
+   npx wrangler secret put FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON
+   # paste the full JSON when prompted, then press Enter
+   ```
+
+Keep this file out of git — it's a real credential (same sensitivity as
+`SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`, already handled the same way; see
+`docs/CONNECT_EXISTING_INFRA.md`).
+
+## 4. Sync and rebuild
+
+```bash
+npm install        # picks up @capacitor/push-notifications etc. — see package.json
+npm run build
+cd android
+npm install
+npm run sync        # copies dist/ into www/ and runs `cap sync android`
+npm run build:debug # or open in Android Studio and build a release APK
+```
+
+## 5. Test it
+
+1. Install the freshly built APK on a device, sign in — this triggers the
+   permission prompt (Android 13+) and, once granted, registers the
+   device's token with the backend (`POST /v1/device-tokens`).
+2. From a second account, like/comment on/follow the first account, or
+   send it a DM.
+3. Background the app (don't force-close it — a notification while the
+   app is in the foreground still shows in Activity, just not as a
+   system-tray banner) and confirm the notification appears.
+
+If nothing arrives: check the Worker's logs (`npx wrangler tail`) for
+`[push]`-prefixed errors — the most common causes are a mistyped/partial
+paste of the service-account JSON, or the device's token not having
+reached the backend at all (check for a row in `device_tokens` for that
+user — `docs/migrations/022_device_push_tokens.sql`).
+
+## Launcher icon badge count
+
+`src/utils/useAppBadge.js` keeps the app's launcher icon badge in sync
+with unread notifications + messages, using `@capawesome/capacitor-badge`
+— no Firebase project needed for this part, it works independently of
+the steps above. Whether it's actually visible depends on the phone's own
+launcher: most OEM launchers (Samsung, Xiaomi, OnePlus, etc.) show the
+real number; stock/Pixel-style (AOSP) launchers only ever show a plain
+dot, tied to whether there's an active system notification rather than a
+number an app can set directly. That's a platform limitation, not
+something this integration can work around.
diff --git a/docs/migrations/022_device_push_tokens.sql b/docs/migrations/022_device_push_tokens.sql
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b356713
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/migrations/022_device_push_tokens.sql
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+-- Migration 022: device push tokens, for real OS-level notifications
+-- (system tray, outside the app) plus the launcher icon's numeric badge.
+-- See backend/src/lib/push.js for how these get used and
+-- docs/PUSH_NOTIFICATIONS_SETUP.md for the Firebase project setup this
+-- depends on.
+--
+-- `token` is the primary key rather than a separate `id` + a unique
+-- constraint on (user_id, token): a push token identifies one specific
+-- app installation on one specific device, not a person, and the same
+-- device can only ever usefully hold one row. That also makes device
+-- handoff between accounts (sign out of one account, sign into another
+-- on the same phone) a plain upsert-on-conflict(token) that reassigns
+-- user_id — see routes/pushTokens.js for why that upsert goes through
+-- the admin client rather than the caller's own RLS-scoped one.
+create table if not exists device_tokens (
+  token text primary key,
+  user_id uuid not null references users(id) on delete cascade,
+  platform text not null default 'android' check (platform in ('android', 'ios', 'web')),
+  created_at timestamptz not null default now(),
+  updated_at timestamptz not null default now()
+);
+
+create index if not exists device_tokens_user_idx on device_tokens(user_id);
+
+alter table device_tokens enable row level security;
+
+-- No insert/upsert policy for `authenticated` here on purpose: the
+-- device-handoff case above needs to reassign a row someone else's
+-- account currently owns, which RLS's `using` clause would otherwise
+-- correctly forbid — routes/pushTokens.js's POST handler uses the
+-- admin client for that write instead, the same escape hatch
+-- lib/notify.js already uses for its own fan-out inserts. Ordinary
+-- reads/deletes of a token you already own don't need that, so those
+-- stay on the normal RLS-scoped path.
+drop policy if exists "users read their own device tokens" on device_tokens;
+create policy "users read their own device tokens"
+  on device_tokens for select using (auth.uid() = user_id);
+
+drop policy if exists "users delete their own device tokens" on device_tokens;
+create policy "users delete their own device tokens"
+  on device_tokens for delete using (auth.uid() = user_id);
+
+grant select, delete on public.device_tokens to authenticated;
